<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Shared manifest. Flavor overlays live in src/foss and src/play.

  Permission inventory is §13 of the requirements corpus, and FR-941 requires every declared
  permission to have an in-app explanation reachable from settings — see
  dev.rsm.ui.settings.PermissionExplanations, which is keyed by the exact strings below. Adding a
  permission here without adding it there is a defect.

  Deliberately absent:
    * android.permission.INTERNET          — FR-607: no core function needs a network, and the Play
                                             build should declare no network permission at all.
    * WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE               — FR-940, forbidden outright.
    * android:requestLegacyExternalStorage — FR-940, forbidden outright.
  There is no `package` attribute: the namespace is declared in build.gradle.kts (AGP requirement).
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!-- FR-604: long-running transfers and mount keepalive run in a foreground service. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_DATA_SYNC" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_SPECIAL_USE" />

    <!-- FR-604's progress notification. Runtime grant on API 33+. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!--
      FR-820/FR-821: declared in both flavors, but the app MUST be fully functional without it.
      The Play build needs the Permissions Declaration Form; if that is refused, the app degrades to
      SAF plus its own DocumentsProvider (FR-202). FR-712 surfaces it as a remedy on HyperOS where
      SAF grants are reported to fail on SD cards.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        tools:ignore="ScopedStorage" />

    <!-- USB host mode. Not required: the app is useful for internal microSD volumes alone. -->
    <uses-feature
        android:name="android.hardware.usb.host"
        android:required="false" />

    <application
        android:name=".RsmApplication"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:icon="@drawable/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Rsm">

        <activity
            android:name=".ui.MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/app_name"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Rsm">

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
              FR-100: attachment is detected by this filter, with a device_filter matching USB
              class 0x08 (Mass Storage). The broadcast fires only on the attach edge, which is why
              UsbDeviceEnumerator also walks UsbManager.getDeviceList() at launch.

              FR-721: where other apps also handle this action the platform shows a disambiguation
              chooser. That is normal and the app must not try to suppress it.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.hardware.usb.action.USB_DEVICE_ATTACHED" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.hardware.usb.action.USB_DEVICE_ATTACHED"
                android:resource="@xml/usb_device_filter" />
        </activity>

        <!--
          NOT YET DECLARED — the classes below do not exist yet, and a manifest component whose class
          is missing is a hard crash, not a no-op. A <provider> is the worst case: ActivityThread
          instantiates every declared provider inside handleBindApplication, before any activity
          runs, so a missing provider class kills the process on every cold start with
          "Unable to get provider ... ClassNotFoundException" and no screen ever appears. Clearing
          app data cannot help, because nothing about it is state-dependent.

          Re-add each block below in the same commit that adds its class — never ahead of it.

          1. FR-500 dev.rsm.provider.RsmDocumentsProvider — the primary interoperability surface on
             both access paths (C-6):

               <provider android:name=".provider.RsmDocumentsProvider"
                   android:authorities="${applicationId}.documents"
                   android:exported="true"
                   android:grantUriPermissions="true"
                   android:permission="android.permission.MANAGE_DOCUMENTS">
                   <intent-filter>
                       <action android:name="android.content.action.DOCUMENTS_PROVIDER" />
                   </intent-filter>
               </provider>

             FR-934 asks that the provider not be exported to arbitrary callers "beyond the SAF
             contract". A provider SAF can enumerate must be exported; what makes that safe is the
             android.permission.MANAGE_DOCUMENTS guard, which only the system holds, plus per-call
             URI grant checks in the provider itself. exported="false" would make the provider
             invisible to SAF and defeat FR-500. To be recorded in
             docs/decisions/0006-provider-export.md.

          2. FR-604 dev.rsm.service.TransferService — bounded work; dataSync is permitted for
             transfers (FR-604a, MAY):

               <service android:name=".service.TransferService"
                   android:exported="false"
                   android:foregroundServiceType="dataSync" />

          3. FR-604a dev.rsm.service.MountKeepaliveService — an unbounded mount keepalive cannot use
             dataSync because of the 6h/24h cumulative cap reported for Android 15+, so it declares
             specialUse with a subtype (@string/fgs_special_use_subtype is already defined). Q4 is
             still open on whether that cap applies as stated; dev.rsm.service.FgsTypePolicy is
             intended to be the single place the choice is made, so resolving Q4 stays a one-file
             change:

               <service android:name=".service.MountKeepaliveService"
                   android:exported="false"
                   android:foregroundServiceType="specialUse">
                   <property android:name="android.app.PROPERTY_SPECIAL_USE_FGS_SUBTYPE"
                       android:value="@string/fgs_special_use_subtype" />
               </service>
        -->
    </application>
</manifest>
